WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:07.706
Begin by framing Introduction to Machine
Learning Workflow as a question about inputs,

2
00:00:07.706 --> 00:00:10.062
decisions, and observable results.

3
00:00:10.062 --> 00:00:17.125
Machine-learning workflows separate data
preparation, training, validation, testing,

4
00:00:17.125 --> 00:00:20.656
model selection, and evaluation.

5
00:00:20.656 --> 00:00:28.804
Consider this worked case: A developer uses a
pipeline to standardize features, train a

6
00:00:28.804 --> 00:00:34.624
classifier, and evaluate accuracy without
mixing training and testing data.

7
00:00:34.624 --> 00:00:41.945
List the givens before choosing a method,
because an unstated assumption can change the

8
00:00:41.945 --> 00:00:42.468
conclusion.

9
00:00:42.468 --> 00:00:48.390
This establishes the vocabulary and scope
needed for the remaining sections.

10
00:00:48.506 --> 00:00:54.841
Now define Data Preparation and Leakage
Prevention precisely enough that another

11
00:00:54.841 --> 00:00:57.721
learner could test the definition.

12
00:00:57.721 --> 00:01:04.659
Preprocessing must be fitted only on permitted
training data to avoid leakage.

13
00:01:04.659 --> 00:01:11.153
Consider this worked case: Scaling features
using the mean and standard deviation

14
00:01:11.153 --> 00:01:16.565
calculated solely from the training set before
splitting the data.

15
00:01:16.565 --> 00:01:23.291
Separate the formal definition from an
implementation detail, then identify what

16
00:01:23.291 --> 00:01:25.737
evidence would distinguish them.

17
00:01:25.737 --> 00:01:32.315
A precise definition makes later examples
easier to evaluate and revise.

18
00:01:32.426 --> 00:01:39.443
With the foundation in place, examine how
Supervised vs. Unsupervised Learning operates

19
00:01:39.443 --> 00:01:41.782
in a concrete sequence.

20
00:01:41.782 --> 00:01:47.797
Supervised methods learn a mapping from
examples paired with target labels.

21
00:01:47.797 --> 00:01:56.547
Consider this worked case: Classifying email
as spam or ham uses labeled data, whereas

22
00:01:56.547 --> 00:02:01.547
clustering customers by purchasing behavior
uses unlabeled data.

23
00:02:01.547 --> 00:02:08.704
Follow every arrow in order and name the state
before and after each transition.

24
00:02:08.704 --> 00:02:14.454
The trace converts an abstract statement into
an inspectable sequence.

25
00:02:14.570 --> 00:02:20.806
Next, compare the choices involved in
Statistical Analysis and Data Visualization

26
00:02:20.806 --> 00:02:24.207
and make the decision criteria explicit.

27
00:02:24.207 --> 00:02:30.438
Topics include: Mathematical background of
machine learning (for example statistical

28
00:02:30.438 --> 00:02:36.670
analysis and visualization of data), Bayesian
decision theory, parametric and

29
00:02:36.670 --> 00:02:43.524
non-parameteric classification models (for
example, SVMs and Nearest Neighbor models) and

30
00:02:43.524 --> 00:02:49.756
neural network models (for example
Convolutional, Recurrent, and Deep Neural

31
00:02:49.756 --> 00:02:50.379
Networks).

32
00:02:50.379 --> 00:02:57.707
Consider this worked case: Plotting a
histogram and box plot to detect outliers and

33
00:02:57.707 --> 00:03:00.848
skewness before selecting a regression model.

34
00:03:00.848 --> 00:03:07.946
Compare alternatives against the same
constraints so the evaluation does not move

35
00:03:07.946 --> 00:03:09.129
the goalposts.

36
00:03:09.129 --> 00:03:15.816
Using shared criteria turns a preference into
a defensible technical choice.

37
00:03:15.938 --> 00:03:23.318
Apply Bayesian Decision Theory to a worked
situation instead of treating it as an

38
00:03:23.318 --> 00:03:24.372
isolated definition.

39
00:03:24.372 --> 00:03:31.899
The NIST AI Risk Management Framework
organizes work around Govern, Map, Measure,

40
00:03:31.899 --> 00:03:33.153
and Manage.

41
00:03:33.153 --> 00:03:41.335
Consider this worked case: Calculating the
posterior probability of a medical diagnosis

42
00:03:41.335 --> 00:03:48.153
given a test result, using prior prevalence
and test sensitivity/specificity.

43
00:03:48.153 --> 00:03:55.961
Work from the concrete inputs to the result,
checking units, types, or invariants at each

44
00:03:55.961 --> 00:03:56.481
step.

45
00:03:56.481 --> 00:04:01.434
The worked case shows both how to apply the
method and how to check it.

46
00:04:01.538 --> 00:04:07.577
Examine the tradeoffs in Parametric
Classification Models, including what improves

47
00:04:07.577 --> 00:04:10.597
and what becomes more difficult.

48
00:04:10.597 --> 00:04:16.676
An introduction to both foundational and
modern machine learning theories and

49
00:04:16.676 --> 00:04:21.097
algorithms, and their application in
classification and regression.

50
00:04:21.097 --> 00:04:30.190
Consider this worked case: Training an SVM on
a dataset of handwritten digits to draw a

51
00:04:30.190 --> 00:04:34.737
hyperplane that separates correct from
incorrect digit classifications.

52
00:04:34.738 --> 00:04:41.644
State the benefit, the cost, and the condition
under which the tradeoff is acceptable.

53
00:04:41.644 --> 00:04:48.987
Making the tradeoff visible prevents a local
improvement from hiding a larger cost.

54
00:04:49.106 --> 00:04:54.526
Stress-test the reasoning around
Non-Parametric Classification Models with an

55
00:04:54.526 --> 00:04:59.946
edge case and identify which assumption
controls the outcome.

56
00:04:59.946 --> 00:05:06.087
Unsupervised methods identify structure
without target labels.

57
00:05:06.087 --> 00:05:13.596
Consider this worked case: Assigning a new
customer to a segment by finding the three

58
00:05:13.596 --> 00:05:19.102
closest historical customers in feature space
and taking their majority class.

59
00:05:19.102 --> 00:05:25.634
Change one assumption at a time and observe
which part of the reasoning no longer holds.

60
00:05:25.634 --> 00:05:32.462
The edge case reveals the boundary of the
claim without discarding the useful core idea.

61
00:05:32.570 --> 00:05:40.503
Use Neural Network Architectures to diagnose a
failure by separating symptoms, causes, and

62
00:05:40.503 --> 00:05:41.723
corrective action.

63
00:05:41.723 --> 00:05:46.613
Risk analysis considers data quality and
uncertainty.

64
00:05:46.613 --> 00:05:52.319
Consider this worked case: Using a
Convolutional Neural Network to detect edges

65
00:05:52.319 --> 00:06:00.403
and textures in images, or a Recurrent Neural
Network to predict the next word in a sentence

66
00:06:00.403 --> 00:06:00.879
sequence.

67
00:06:00.879 --> 00:06:08.419
Locate the first point where observed behavior
diverges from expected behavior, then test the

68
00:06:08.419 --> 00:06:10.035
smallest plausible cause.

69
00:06:10.035 --> 00:06:17.754
The diagnosis is strongest when another person
can reproduce the observation and the test.

70
00:06:17.858 --> 00:06:24.355
Connect Model Evaluation and Hyperparameter
Selection to the earlier ideas and explain how

71
00:06:24.355 --> 00:06:27.354
the pieces work as one system.

72
00:06:27.354 --> 00:06:33.886
Evaluation metrics must match the task and the
cost of different errors.

73
00:06:33.886 --> 00:06:41.378
Consider this worked case: Using precision and
recall for imbalanced classification tasks,

74
00:06:41.378 --> 00:06:48.245
and tuning learning rate via cross-validation
before testing on held-out data.

75
00:06:48.245 --> 00:06:57.794
Explain each connection explicitly and verify
that information retains the same meaning

76
00:06:57.794 --> 00:06:59.386
across representations.

77
00:06:59.386 --> 00:07:06.417
The synthesis matters only if every connection
remains supported by the original evidence.

78
00:07:06.530 --> 00:07:13.600
Finish with Practical Analysis Checklist as a
practical checklist that can guide a new

79
00:07:13.600 --> 00:07:14.105
analysis.

80
00:07:14.105 --> 00:07:21.761
It also considers robustness, privacy,
security, transparency, and fairness.

81
00:07:21.761 --> 00:07:28.497
Consider this worked case: Before deployment,
verify that preprocessing parameters were not

82
00:07:28.497 --> 00:07:35.795
fitted on test data, check for bias in
predictions across demographic groups, and

83
00:07:35.795 --> 00:07:37.479
schedule post-deployment monitoring.

84
00:07:37.480 --> 00:07:45.293
For each checklist item, record the evidence,
the remaining uncertainty, and the next

85
00:07:45.293 --> 00:07:46.495
verification step.

86
00:07:46.495 --> 00:07:53.320
The result is a repeatable procedure, not a
conclusion that depends on memory or

87
00:07:53.320 --> 00:07:53.808
intuition.
